purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135387380,imgsize-96048.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[11-year-old Egyptian girl beats 40 million students in reading contest; what Dubai ruler did next won hearts]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135385994.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Mayar Othman Al Shourbagy, an 11-year-old student from Egypt, won the 10th Arab Reading Challenge. Sheikh Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum presented her with a badge to commemorate her achievement. She read 60 books as part of her preparation for the competition. The challenge attracted over 40 million participants from 60 countries, promoting a culture of reading. Mayar's family and school supported her throughout her reading journey.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/11-year-old-egyptian-girl-beats-40-million-students-in-reading-contest-what-dubai-ruler-sheikh-mohammed-did-after-her-win-caught-attention/articleshow/135385994.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/11-year-old-egyptian-girl-beats-40-million-students-in-reading-contest-what-dubai-ruler-sheikh-mohammed-did-after-her-win-caught-attention/articleshow/135385994.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-31T08:51:03+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Lifestyle Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135386078,imgsize-754602.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Chuck Lorre-backed LA charity campus breaks ground on final building after kitchen opening]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135359933.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[On October 29, 2026, Project Angel Food initiated construction of the Community Outreach Center in Hollywood. This new building will enhance client services and nutrition education for individuals facing serious illnesses. The project, supported by The Chuck Lorre Family Foundation, aims to expand meal preparation and outreach programs. As part of a multiyear campaign, the expanded facilities will elevate the organisation's role in the Food Is Medicine movement.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/big-bang-theory-creator-chuck-lorres-los-angeles-charity-campus-broke-ground-on-its-final-building-on-october-29-after-opening-a-new-kitchen-in-february-as-its-nonprofit-delivers-1-5-million-meals-a-year/articleshow/135359933.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/big-bang-theory-creator-chuck-lorres-los-angeles-charity-campus-broke-ground-on-its-final-building-on-october-29-after-opening-a-new-kitchen-in-february-as-its-nonprofit-delivers-1-5-million-meals-a-year/articleshow/135359933.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-31T08:50:43+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135360213,imgsize-1945079.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[A first-grade teacher in Kansas started ending each day with children repeating positive affirmations; the simple routine spread beyond his classroom as other teachers adopted the idea]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135239944.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[An elementary teacher in Kansas has implemented positive affirmations at the end of each school day. This practice has spread across various grades, enhancing students' sense of belonging and value. While affirmations alone do not replace effective teaching methods, they contribute to a supportive classroom atmosphere. Parents can also foster encouragement in their children by acknowledging their feelings and individuality.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/first-grade-teacher-in-kansas-started-ending-each-day-with-children-repeating-positive-affirmations-the-simple-routine-spread-beyond-his-classroom-as-other-teachers-adopted-the-idea/articleshow/135239944.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/first-grade-teacher-in-kansas-started-ending-each-day-with-children-repeating-positive-affirmations-the-simple-routine-spread-beyond-his-classroom-as-other-teachers-adopted-the-idea/articleshow/135239944.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-31T07:55:49+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Lifestyle Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135351882,imgsize-1103571.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Indiana landlord loses $3,800 in apartment damage claim after failing to send notice]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135354690.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[An Indiana landlord who won $6,200 from a former tenant over unpaid rent and apartment damage has had the award reduced to $2,400. In an October 29 ruling, the state appeals court found that the landlord had failed to provide the tenant with a written list of damages as required by law. The court allowed the unpaid rent claim but rejected the additional cleaning and repair costs.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/an-indiana-landlord-won-6200-from-a-former-tenant-after-claiming-extensive-apartment-damage-on-october-29-an-appeals-court-cut-the-award-to-2400-because-the-owner-never-sent-a-required-damage-list/articleshow/135354690.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/an-indiana-landlord-won-6200-from-a-former-tenant-af